WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.700
SPEAKER: In our last episode,
we covered the best practices

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.670
for deploying and using cloud
operations in an enterprise

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:06.720
environment.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:09.240
But we still left some
questions unanswered.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.580
How should you
monitor your services?

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:13.170
How should you deal with alerts?

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:14.970
And what about managing costs?

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:16.890
Today, we'll look
at best practices

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:20.070
that you can use to get the most
value out of cloud monitoring.

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:22.980
Welcome to Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud.

00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:29.120
Just like last time,
let's first figure out

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.190
why monitoring is
important at all.

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:34.070
What are we trying to achieve
when we use cloud monitoring?

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:37.100
We're after three main
objectives in parallel.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:39.560
Our first objective is
to keep our users happy.

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:41.690
That means we need to know
whether our service is

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:42.830
meeting their needs.

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:45.770
And the best way to do that
is through well-defined SLOs

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:47.660
and error budgets,
and alerts that

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:51.050
let us know when our service
is burning error budget.

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:53.930
Our second objective is to be
able to quickly and effectively

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:56.720
respond when those
alerts do fire.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:59.540
This means having enough data
about the state of our service

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:03.200
to be able to triage incidents,
identify effective mitigations,

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.450
and accurately confirm
the effectiveness

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:07.340
of those mitigations.

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:10.310
Our final goal is to do the
first two while managing

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:12.380
the associated costs.

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:15.680
Now that we've covered the
whys, let's turn to the hows.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:18.590
We'll start by talking
about what to monitor.

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:21.440
From there, we'll get into
how to best manage alerts.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:23.690
And finally, we'll
cover cost management

00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:25.010
for cloud monitoring.

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:27.357
Let's get into it.

00:01:27.357 --> 00:01:29.690
Let's start by talking about
the obvious first question.

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:31.430
What should you be monitoring?

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:33.830
For the answer, we
can turn to chapter 6

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:36.560
of the "Site Reliability
Engineering" book on monitoring

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:38.180
distributed systems.

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:40.100
Fundamentally, the
objective of monitoring

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:41.790
is to answer two questions.

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:43.850
The first one is
all about symptoms.

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:46.820
What are our users experiencing,
and is that experience

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:49.220
in any way less than ideal?

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:51.620
The second question
is all about causes.

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:54.140
What's causing the
symptoms we've identified?

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:56.600
That cause may be
direct or intermediate,

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:58.070
but it's how we
start to determine

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:01.190
how to restore user experience
back to the desired state

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:03.470
if it's degraded in some way.

00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:06.950
We answer the first question by
clearly defining our service,

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:10.370
identifying good service
level indicators, or SLIs

00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:12.680
that tell us, as
accurately as possible,

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:16.040
how well our service is able to
meet our users' expectations,

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:19.940
and defining good service
level objectives, or SLOs, that

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:24.670
set a target that our
SLIs have to meet.

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:27.520
We've covered defining
services, SLIs, and SLOs

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:29.890
in cloud monitoring
extensively in the series,

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:31.872
but it's always worth
revisiting these topics.

00:02:31.872 --> 00:02:34.330
The first thing to answer to
the question of what should we

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:36.730
monitor is, how
well is your service

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:41.613
meeting the needs of your users
as expressed by good SLOs?

00:02:41.613 --> 00:02:43.780
The next thing to make sure
you're monitoring covers

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:47.080
is what the "SRE" book refers
to as the golden signals.

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:49.360
Traffic is essentially
a measure of how much

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:52.120
user activity the service
is responding to, usually

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:55.450
as a count of incoming requests
for user-facing services

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:58.220
or data elements
for data pipelines.

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:01.070
Error rate is a measure of
what percentage of the time

00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:03.230
the user activity
is not successful,

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:07.120
which, of course, depends on the
service and user expectations.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.730
Latency is a measure of
how long the service takes

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:11.260
to service a request.

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:13.780
And finally,
saturation is a measure

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:15.680
of how much of the
available capacity

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:17.470
the service is using
at a given time.

00:03:19.952 --> 00:03:21.410
A great way to
ensure that you have

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:24.200
easy access to these signals
is to create a dashboard

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:25.250
like this.

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:28.460
It shows the four golden signals
for a given service at once.

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:30.920
It makes it easy to correlate,
for example, increases

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:34.100
in inbound traffic to latency
degradations or increased

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:35.840
capacity requirements.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:37.760
You could automate the
creation of dashboards

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:39.380
like this for your services.

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:43.170
We'll link to some examples
in the episode notes.

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:45.260
Finally, you may need to
augment your monitoring

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:48.770
with custom telemetry to capture
service-specific information.

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:50.687
We covered doing this
in a previous episode.

00:03:50.687 --> 00:03:53.270
Review that for an introduction
to instrumenting your services

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:55.850
with OpenCensus and Prometheus.

00:03:55.850 --> 00:03:57.560
So that takes care of
the first question,

00:03:57.560 --> 00:03:59.060
what you should be monitoring.

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:03.140
We've just covered SLOs, golden
signals, and custom telemetry,

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:05.090
which brings us to alerting.

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:06.500
How should you configure alerts?

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:07.700
What should you alert on?

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:09.900
And how should you
respond to alerts?

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:12.150
Let's take a look.

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:14.580
Let's again return to the
monitoring chapter of the "SRE"

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:15.258
book.

00:04:15.258 --> 00:04:16.800
These four criteria
should be applied

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:18.870
to alerts that go to pagers.

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:20.790
Every time the
pager goes off, we

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:23.010
should be able to react
with a sense of urgency.

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:25.140
But we can only react
with a sense of urgency

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:28.660
a few times a day before
we become fatigued.

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:31.090
Every page should be actionable.

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:33.520
Every page response should
require intelligence.

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:35.670
If a page merits only
a robotic response,

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:37.660
it shouldn't be a page.

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:41.290
And ideally, pages alert
us about a novel problem

00:04:41.290 --> 00:04:43.330
or an event that hasn't
been seen before.

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:44.710
That means we need
to take action

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:46.840
to prevent pages from
reoccurring once we've

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:48.610
mitigated the
triggering condition

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:51.350
and, hopefully,
identified the root cause.

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:54.370
Alerts that don't meet these
criteria should not be pages.

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:57.560
Alerts that aren't actionable
are of no use at all.

00:04:57.560 --> 00:04:59.470
Alerts that require
human intervention

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:03.250
but that aren't urgent should
be filed as tickets instead.

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:06.040
Cloud monitoring supports
multiple notification channels.

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:08.170
And this allows you to
set up alert delivery that

00:05:08.170 --> 00:05:09.410
meets your needs.

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:11.950
For example, you could use
email or PagerDuty delivery

00:05:11.950 --> 00:05:13.210
to trigger pagers.

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:15.340
Then configure a webhook
or Pub/Sub integration

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:19.455
to file tickets for
non-urgent alerts.

00:05:19.455 --> 00:05:21.580
You can also automate the
management of your alerts

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:23.950
by using an automation
tool like Terraform.

00:05:23.950 --> 00:05:27.130
This helps to ensure consistency
and avoids human errors

00:05:27.130 --> 00:05:29.468
in alert configuration.

00:05:29.468 --> 00:05:31.010
Finally, it's
important to understand

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:33.440
the tools you have available
to capture and optimize

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:34.910
your monitoring costs.

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:38.482
Let's have a look at those now.

00:05:38.482 --> 00:05:39.940
First, it's important
to understand

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:42.130
how you incur monitoring costs.

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:44.530
Platform metrics emitted
by Google Cloud Services

00:05:44.530 --> 00:05:45.880
are always free.

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:47.410
You are charged
for other metrics,

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:50.260
such as custom metrics or
those written by Istio,

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:52.043
based on storage volume.

00:05:52.043 --> 00:05:53.710
Refer to the Pricing
page for the latest

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:55.630
version of this information.

00:05:55.630 --> 00:05:58.720
The Monitoring Settings page
shows you exactly how much data

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:01.840
you are ingesting for each
project in your metrics scope.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:04.570
You can measure your usage
by using billing reports

00:06:04.570 --> 00:06:07.210
and filtering them to just
the monitoring service

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:09.430
to see exactly how much
monitoring charges you

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:11.440
are incurring in your project.

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:13.150
You can even figure
out exactly how much

00:06:13.150 --> 00:06:15.730
data is being ingested
by each custom metric

00:06:15.730 --> 00:06:19.330
by creating a chart and using
the Metric Bytes Ingested

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:20.900
metric.

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:22.490
These tools do a
great job of helping

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:25.040
you understand the sources
of monitoring costs.

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:26.810
But what can you do
to optimize those?

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:29.800
Let's have a look.

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:31.870
The way that you use
labels for cloud components

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:33.820
might impact the
volume of time series

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:36.580
that are generated for your
metrics and monitoring.

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:38.890
For example, you can use
labels to appropriately

00:06:38.890 --> 00:06:41.020
report metrics to cost
centers on your bill

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:43.840
and to signify whether specific
environments are production

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:45.070
or development.

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:47.860
Adding these labels means
that additional time series

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:49.480
are generated in monitoring.

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:51.940
You can calculate the total
number of times series

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:55.000
by multiplying the
cardinality of all labels.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:57.190
For example, if there
are 11 cost center

00:06:57.190 --> 00:07:00.010
values and 5
environment values, that

00:07:00.010 --> 00:07:02.870
means 55 time series
are generated.

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:05.260
This is why the way that
you add labels might

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:07.660
add significant metric
volume, and, therefore,

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:09.260
increase the cost.

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:11.800
We recommend carefully
limiting the number of labels

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:15.390
and avoiding label values
with high cardinality.

00:07:15.390 --> 00:07:17.100
Metrics sent from
the Cloud Ops Agent

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:19.080
are another source
of monitoring costs.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:21.330
The agent collects
app and system metrics

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:25.410
from common third-party apps
and additional VM-level metrics.

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:27.090
If you don't need
them for certain VMs,

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:30.160
you can reduce the volume by
not sending these metrics.

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:32.040
You can also reduce
the metric values

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:35.580
by reducing the number of
VMs that use the agent.

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:37.350
Custom metrics are
chargeable metrics

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:40.290
created by using the
monitoring API or integrations

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:42.330
like OpenCensus to
monitor any metrics that

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:43.830
are user instruments.

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:46.480
The more services that you
instrument to send metrics,

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:49.540
the more custom monitoring
metrics are generated.

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:51.180
If you want to reduce
metric volumes,

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:53.430
you can reduce the number
of custom monitoring metrics

00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:56.170
that your services send.

00:07:56.170 --> 00:07:57.562
Thanks for joining me today.

00:07:57.562 --> 00:07:59.770
We've covered best practices
for setting up and using

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:02.080
cloud monitoring,
including what to monitor,

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:05.590
how to manage alerts, and how to
optimize your monitoring costs.

00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:08.560
I hope these recommendations
help you keep your users happy.

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:11.110
In our next episode, we'll
tackle best practices

00:08:11.110 --> 00:08:12.490
for using cloud logging.

00:08:12.490 --> 00:08:14.180
Thanks so much for
watching today.

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:16.660
Don't forget to like and
subscribe to never miss out

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:19.990
on more Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud.

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:20.920
See you soon.

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:24.270
[MUSIC PLAYING]

